    {
      "id": "pu1pzpSAraLJZd-0nmVtk",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 1853.1708972911988,
      "y": 3255.348272679071,
      "width": 542.819580078125,
      "height": 575,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b40",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1375205420,
      "version": 65,
      "versionNonce": 1697349908,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1790602978434,
      "created": 1790602856138,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Think of the diagram as the complete life cycle of\na process. When a process is created, it first enters \nthe New state. The OS then moves it to Ready, \nmeaning it is ready to run but is waiting for CPU time.\nWhen the CPU Scheduler gives it the CPU, it moves \nto Running. From Running, three main things can \nhappen: if the process finishes its work, it goes \nto Terminated; if it needs an I/O operation such \nas reading a file, waiting for a network response,\nor accessing a database, it moves to \nWaiting/Blocked, and once the I/O is complete, \nit returns to Ready; or, if its CPU time slice \nexpires or the OS preempts it, it moves back to \nReady so another process can use the CPU. \nThe Suspended/Ready and Suspended/Wait \nstates are more advanced states where the OS \ntemporarily removes a process from main memory \n(swaps it out); Suspended/Ready means \nit is ready to run but currently not in memory, \nwhile Suspended/Wait means it is both waiting \nfor an event/I/O and swapped out.\nSo the core cycle to remember is: \nNew → Ready → Running → Waiting/Ready/Terminated.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "baseFontSize": null,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Think of the diagram as the complete life cycle of\na process. When a process is created, it first enters \nthe New state. The OS then moves it to Ready, \nmeaning it is ready to run but is waiting for CPU time.\nWhen the CPU Scheduler gives it the CPU, it moves \nto Running. From Running, three main things can \nhappen: if the process finishes its work, it goes \nto Terminated; if it needs an I/O operation such \nas reading a file, waiting for a network response,\nor accessing a database, it moves to \nWaiting/Blocked, and once the I/O is complete, \nit returns to Ready; or, if its CPU time slice \nexpires or the OS preempts it, it moves back to \nReady so another process can use the CPU. \nThe Suspended/Ready and Suspended/Wait \nstates are more advanced states where the OS \ntemporarily removes a process from main memory \n(swaps it out); Suspended/Ready means \nit is ready to run but currently not in memory, \nwhile Suspended/Wait means it is both waiting \nfor an event/I/O and swapped out.\nSo the core cycle to remember is: \nNew → Ready → Running → Waiting/Ready/Terminated.",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null
    },
